  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Code Structure**\n\nI\'ve just finished studying the codebase structure in detail. I have reviewed `types.ts`, `data.ts`, and the main components including `App.tsx`, `Header.tsx`, `TestSection.tsx`, `CalculatorSection.tsx`, `MotivationGuide.tsx`, `FAQSection.tsx`, and `ConsultationModal.tsx`. I believe a good understanding is now in place.\n\n\n**Implementing Geometric Balance**\n\nI\'m now focusing on implementing the \"Geometric Balance\" design. I\'ve begun to translate the design specifications into actionable steps. My focus is on extracting styling and layout patterns, avoiding any modification to the existing HTML structure. I\'m prioritizing the specified colors and aesthetic characteristics (minimalist, serif fonts, geometric shapes). I\'ll start with declaring the key colors in the code.\n\n\n**Defining Theme Variables**\n\nI\'ve decided to define CSS custom properties, utilizing Tailwind CSS v4 variables within `/src/index.css`. This includes importing font families and establishing a set of variables for the \"Geometric Balance\" theme. I\'ve defined the primary and secondary background colors, text colors, accent colors, and border colors. Now I\'m considering mapping these variables to existing Tailwind semantic classes to optimize utility.\n\n\n**Mapping Tailwind Classes**\n\nNow I\'m looking at how I can map the new theme variables to existing Tailwind utility classes. I\'ve analyzed the current codebase for background, text, accent, and border colors. I have listed the Tailwind classes that are currently in use, e.g. `bg-stone-950`, `text-stone-300`, etc. I\'m considering using Tailwind v4\'s `@theme` configuration to replace these or to map them directly to the variables I\'ve created. This could streamline the codebase.\n\n\n**Refining Color Aliases**\n\nI\'m now refining the color aliases and thinking about how to apply them. With Tailwind v4, I\'m leaning toward defining `--color-*` variables to override or supplement existing color palettes. I\'m focusing on a light-theme layout inspired by \"Geometric Balance\". I\'m specifying the primary background as `#FDFCF8`, text as `#3A3F33`, and accent as `#6B705C`. Borders will be `#E8E6DE`, secondary text is `#B7B7A4`, hover backgrounds `#F8F7F2`, and the footer accent is `#A5A58D`.\n\n\n**Styling Geometric Balance**\n\nI\'m now determining the most effective method for styling the existing HTML to reflect the \"Geometric Balance\" theme. I\'m weighing options such as overriding Tailwind classes, creating custom CSS variables in `/src/index.css`, and defining semantic custom classes. The aim is to map the new theme colors to existing utility classes like `bg-stone-950` or to define equivalents like `bg-brand-bg`. I\'m also exploring Tailwind v4\'s `@theme` directive for color variable definition, avoiding modifications to component-level HTML.\n\n\n**Refining CSS Strategies**\n\nI\'m now reconsidering the approach for mapping colors. Overriding standard Tailwind color names directly could introduce confusion regarding contrast. I think it\'s cleaner to define new, custom variables and utility classes, then selectively apply them to the components. My focus is on robust styling and maintainability.\n\n\n**Defining Theme Variables**\n\nI\'ve made progress refining the color palette for the \"Geometric Balance\" theme and am now using Tailwind v4\'s `@theme` directive in `index.css`. I\'ve defined primary and secondary background colors, along with text, accent, and border colors. I\'ve also declared a serif font family. This will allow for more streamlined styling, so I\'m also now adding some utility variable definitions for increased styling flexibility.\n\n\n**Implementing Utility Classes**\n\nI\'m now generating utility classes based on my theme variables, as planned. I\'m focusing on those specific to the \"Geometric Balance\" design. This includes the following, among others: `bg-bg-primary`, `text-text-secondary`, and `border-border-custom`. Additionally, I\'m integrating font styles (`font-sans`, `font-serif`). I\'m also ensuring geometric styling with `rounded-none` and `rounded-sm` classes for a crisp visual effect, with more options for interactive elements.\n\n\n"
